declare data_ jsonb:='{"questions":[{"id":"89d1d0f0-0b55-512a-8503-0b6b5c84dcb6","code":"pool-dose","track_id":"est","topic":"Ratios, percentages and unit conversion","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"A pool treatment requires 9 millilitres of concentrate for every 1.2 cubic metres of water. At the same dosing rate, how many millilitres are required for 26 cubic metres of water?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"195"},{"key":"B","text":"202"},{"key":"C","text":"223"},{"key":"D","text":"234"}],"correct":"A","explanation":"A. The dosing rate is 9 ÷ 1.2 = 7.5 millilitres per cubic metre. For 26 cubic metres, 7.5 × 26 = 195 millilitres. These are different quantities related by a dosing rate, not an invalid conversion between gallons and cubic metres.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-pool-dose","source_ids_adapted":["00858c9b-1dd1-5bb3-7ca9-36307cd63d78","5a374899-5d5d-d706-1ce4-77197041133e"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Ratios, percentages and unit conversion","lesson_subtopic":"Direct proportion","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved"},"replaces":["00858c9b-1dd1-5bb3-7ca9-36307cd63d78","5a374899-5d5d-d706-1ce4-77197041133e"]},{"id":"779824f6-5c74-529d-9918-49eaa8d4145d","code":"factor-condition","track_id":"est","topic":"Quadratics and polynomials","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"The polynomial p(x) has factors x − 3 and x + 2, and p(0) = 0. Which expression could be p(x)?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"x³ − x² − 6x"},{"key":"B","text":"x³ + x² − 6x"},{"key":"C","text":"x³ − 5x² + 6x"},{"key":"D","text":"x³ − 7x − 6"}],"correct":"A","explanation":"A. The three required roots are 3, −2 and 0. Choice A factors as x(x − 3)(x + 2). B fails at x = 3, C fails at x = −2, and D has p(0) = −6. The added p(0) = 0 condition makes A uniquely correct.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-factor-condition","source_ids_adapted":["22a42973-8e94-0307-7341-5a98290fdaf9","dba94174-dfce-f518-cbb3-76d958d73bea"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Quadratics and polynomials","lesson_subtopic":"Factors and polynomial roots","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved"},"replaces":["22a42973-8e94-0307-7341-5a98290fdaf9","dba94174-dfce-f518-cbb3-76d958d73bea"]},{"id":"30664b0f-361a-532b-9d76-0f1582d464f1","code":"fair-spinners","track_id":"est","topic":"Probability and conditional probability","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"Two fair spinners have equal-sized sectors. Spinner 1 has sectors labeled 1, 2, 3, 4, and Spinner 2 has sectors labeled 1, 2, 3. They are spun independently once each. What is the probability that the product is even?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"1/4"},{"key":"B","text":"1/3"},{"key":"C","text":"1/2"},{"key":"D","text":"2/3"},{"key":"E","text":"3/4"}],"correct":"D","explanation":"D. There are 4 × 3 = 12 equally likely ordered sector pairs. An odd product needs both labels odd: 2 × 2 = 4 pairs. Thus P(even product) = 1 − 4/12 = 2/3.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-fair-spinners","source_ids_adapted":["43f410ac-41a2-a7a0-09b7-effd44fca3ea","fb385f7e-c71c-22d8-3c30-05a7ad9269c5"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Probability and conditional probability","lesson_subtopic":"Independent equally likely outcomes","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved"},"replaces":["43f410ac-41a2-a7a0-09b7-effd44fca3ea","fb385f7e-c71c-22d8-3c30-05a7ad9269c5"]},{"id":"389099ff-c93c-5290-9688-3fc4ea0f5ffa","code":"weighted-mean","track_id":"est","topic":"Statistics and data analysis","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"Class A has 30 students with a mean test score of 15.8; Class B has 20 students with a mean of 16.2; Class C has 10 students with a mean of 17.2. One student from each class scored 20. After removing those three scores, what is the mean of the remaining scores, rounded to one decimal place?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"15.1"},{"key":"B","text":"16.0"},{"key":"C","text":"16.2"},{"key":"D","text":"16.7"}],"correct":"B","explanation":"B. The original sum is 30(15.8) + 20(16.2) + 10(17.2) = 970 for 60 students. Removing three scores of 20 leaves 910 for 57 students. The new mean is 910/57 = 15.964912…, which rounds to 16.0, not 15.9.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-weighted-mean","source_ids_adapted":["44d81c11-3760-7638-292b-77268461e4b5","efd7a835-6f42-cb9a-1a06-07232cdab400"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics and data analysis","lesson_subtopic":"Weighted mean after removing observations","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved"},"replaces":["44d81c11-3760-7638-292b-77268461e4b5","efd7a835-6f42-cb9a-1a06-07232cdab400"]},{"id":"099a85b1-62a7-5b1c-a74d-744e6d177c45","code":"replacement-height","track_id":"est","topic":"Statistics and data analysis","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"Three people have heights 160 cm, 170 cm and 180 cm. The person who is 170 cm tall leaves and another person joins. The new mean height is 175 cm. What is the new person’s height?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"165 cm"},{"key":"B","text":"170 cm"},{"key":"C","text":"172 cm"},{"key":"D","text":"185 cm"},{"key":"E","text":"190 cm"}],"correct":"D","explanation":"D. The two remaining people have a total height of 160 + 180 = 340 cm. A mean of 175 for three people requires a total of 525 cm. The newcomer is 525 − 340 = 185 cm tall. The departing person is now explicitly identified.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-replacement-height","source_ids_adapted":["855deeb1-fd4b-3673-3528-a52fde5f19e5","8d191406-f760-fa40-c64e-376509666eb9"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics and data analysis","lesson_subtopic":"Mean with one replacement","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved"},"replaces":["855deeb1-fd4b-3673-3528-a52fde5f19e5","8d191406-f760-fa40-c64e-376509666eb9"]},{"id":"a731fcf9-4304-5b9e-98ff-2e508470fdd2","code":"independent-penalties","track_id":"est","topic":"Probability and conditional probability","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"A player has a 0.3 probability of scoring on each penalty shot. The results of the shots are independent. What is the probability that the player scores on all three of three shots?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"0.027"},{"key":"B","text":"0.343"},{"key":"C","text":"0.540"},{"key":"D","text":"0.90"}],"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Independence allows multiplication: 0.3 × 0.3 × 0.3 = 0.027. Without independence, the individual success probability would not determine the probability of three successes.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-independent-penalties","source_ids_adapted":["9957502d-04d6-c636-3947-31d2ed2ffc04","b7c33115-0e83-acfe-d2e7-e5dd4364c922"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Probability and conditional probability","lesson_subtopic":"Independent repeated trials","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved"},"replaces":["9957502d-04d6-c636-3947-31d2ed2ffc04","b7c33115-0e83-acfe-d2e7-e5dd4364c922"]},{"id":"3095c986-98eb-5279-b33a-991d4a838598","code":"compound-rate","track_id":"est","topic":"Exponents, radicals and growth","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"Nada invests $6,000 in an account with interest compounded annually. After 8 years the balance is $8,950.95. What is the annual interest rate, rounded to the nearest whole percent?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"3%"},{"key":"B","text":"1%"},{"key":"C","text":"5%"},{"key":"D","text":"7%"}],"correct":"C","explanation":"C. If r is the decimal rate, 6000(1 + r)⁸ = 8950.95. Thus r = (8950.95/6000)^(1/8) − 1 ≈ 0.0512711, or 5.12711%. Rounded to the nearest whole percent, this is 5%. At an exact 5% rate the balance would be about $8,864.73; the rounding condition is essential.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-compound-rate","source_ids_adapted":["9f479ecd-a687-b0be-fc29-ace016e04fff","ce2258e9-a83b-a947-3290-7cac213933ca"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Exponents, radicals and growth","lesson_subtopic":"Compound interest and rounding","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved"},"replaces":["9f479ecd-a687-b0be-fc29-ace016e04fff","ce2258e9-a83b-a947-3290-7cac213933ca"]},{"id":"a90c5ace-692f-5209-aa94-5d48703d5018","code":"coefficient-identity","track_id":"est","topic":"Quadratics and polynomials","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"For every real x, (2a − 14)x² + 5b − cx = 2x² − (4x + 5c)b, where a, b and c are constants and b ≠ 0. What is a + 8b + c?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"11"},{"key":"B","text":"7"},{"key":"C","text":"5"},{"key":"D","text":"3"}],"correct":"C","explanation":"C. Comparing coefficients gives 2a − 14 = 2, c = 4b, and 5b = −5bc. Therefore a = 8 and b(1 + c) = 0. Since b ≠ 0, c = −1 and b = −1/4. The value is 8 + 8(−1/4) − 1 = 5. The excluded b = 0 branch would instead give 8.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-coefficient-identity","source_ids_adapted":["38dae603-01c3-93f3-000f-2d8610facd6a","cee9c7d1-307b-60d3-afd0-4347ea1bc2aa"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Quadratics and polynomials","lesson_subtopic":"Matching coefficients with a nonzero condition","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved"},"replaces":["38dae603-01c3-93f3-000f-2d8610facd6a","cee9c7d1-307b-60d3-afd0-4347ea1bc2aa"]},{"id":"7d54a13b-bb06-5718-bbff-83932573a393","code":"intersecting-chords","track_id":"est2","topic":"Circles","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"Points A, B, C and D lie on a circle in that order. Chords AC and BD intersect at P inside the circle. The measure of ∠APB is 80° and the measure of minor arc AB is 60°. What is the measure of minor arc CD?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"20°"},{"key":"B","text":"40°"},{"key":"C","text":"80°"},{"key":"D","text":"100°"}],"correct":"D","explanation":"D. An angle formed by intersecting chords is half the sum of its intercepted opposite arcs. Thus 80 = (60 + m(arc CD))/2, giving m(arc CD) = 160 − 60 = 100°. The chord endpoints and intercepted arcs are explicitly identified.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-intersecting-chords","source_ids_adapted":["6d671cad-0f53-5e10-abaf-6d1328031b81"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Circles","lesson_subtopic":"Angles formed by intersecting chords","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 330\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Intersecting chords AC and BD\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"420\" height=\"330\" fill=\"white\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"165\" r=\"115\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#708196\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"315.00\" cy=\"165.00\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"333.00\" y=\"170.00\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">A</text><circle cx=\"257.50\" cy=\"65.41\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"266.50\" y=\"54.82\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">B</text><circle cx=\"85.00\" cy=\"165.00\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"67.00\" y=\"170.00\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">C</text><circle cx=\"219.97\" cy=\"278.25\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"223.10\" y=\"300.98\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">D</text><line x1=\"315.00\" y1=\"165.00\" x2=\"85.00\" y2=\"165.00\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"257.50\" y1=\"65.41\" x2=\"219.97\" y2=\"278.25\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"239.94\" cy=\"165.00\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"230.94\" y=\"187.00\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">P</text><text x=\"272.94\" y=\"148.00\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">80°</text><text x=\"318.00\" y=\"80.00\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">arc AB = 60°</text></svg>","figure_required":true,"figure_caption":"Instructor-created diagram from the explicit mathematical givens; not a recovered source image."},"replaces":["6d671cad-0f53-5e10-abaf-6d1328031b81"]},{"id":"0a045c5e-457c-567a-8771-4af81d8c27d1","code":"cyclic-quadrilateral","track_id":"est2","topic":"Circles","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"ABCD is a quadrilateral inscribed in a circle. Its interior angle B measures (2y + 10)° and its opposite interior angle D measures (y + 20)°. What is the measure of angle B?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"50°"},{"key":"B","text":"60°"},{"key":"C","text":"80°"},{"key":"D","text":"110°"}],"correct":"D","explanation":"D. Opposite interior angles of an inscribed quadrilateral sum to 180°. Hence (2y + 10) + (y + 20) = 180, so y = 50 and angle B = 110°. Angle D is 70°, confirming a total of 180°. This is a newly specified cyclic-quadrilateral exercise, not a reconstruction of the missing parallel-chord source.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-cyclic-quadrilateral","source_ids_adapted":["e390176d-dc15-5440-8f9c-c89f7706e604"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Circles","lesson_subtopic":"Opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 330\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cyclic quadrilateral ABCD\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"420\" height=\"330\" fill=\"white\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"165\" r=\"115\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#708196\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"100.41\" cy=\"107.50\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"84.82\" y=\"103.50\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">A</text><circle cx=\"111.90\" cy=\"238.92\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"98.12\" y=\"255.49\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">B</text><circle cx=\"239.33\" cy=\"273.06\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"245.49\" y=\"294.98\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">C</text><circle cx=\"315.00\" cy=\"165.00\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"333.00\" y=\"170.00\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">D</text><line x1=\"100.41\" y1=\"107.50\" x2=\"111.90\" y2=\"238.92\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"111.90\" y1=\"238.92\" x2=\"239.33\" y2=\"273.06\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"239.33\" y1=\"273.06\" x2=\"315.00\" y2=\"165.00\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"315.00\" y1=\"165.00\" x2=\"100.41\" y2=\"107.50\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"144.00\" y=\"231.00\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">(2y + 10)°</text><text x=\"274.00\" y=\"164.00\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">(y + 20)°</text></svg>","figure_required":true,"figure_caption":"Instructor-created diagram from the explicit mathematical givens; not a recovered source image."},"replaces":["e390176d-dc15-5440-8f9c-c89f7706e604"]},{"id":"c3eb47cf-ca18-5a8a-8f5d-24a1245644ce","code":"triangle-aa","track_id":"est2","topic":"Triangles and similarity","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"In triangle ACB, D lies between C and B on segment CB, and ∠CAD = ∠ABC. Which statement correctly justifies △ACD ∼ △BCA?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"∠CAD = ∠ABC and AD is a common side"},{"key":"B","text":"∠CAD = ∠ABC and AC is a common side"},{"key":"C","text":"∠CAD = ∠ABC and the angle at C is common"},{"key":"D","text":"None of these"}],"correct":"C","explanation":"C. Since D lies on segment CB, rays CD and CB coincide, so ∠ACD = ∠BCA. Together with the given ∠CAD = ∠ABC, there are two matching angles, proving similarity by AA with correspondence A ↔ B, C ↔ C, D ↔ A. A common side alone does not establish similarity.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-triangle-aa","source_ids_adapted":["75a90933-fefc-5852-a2e5-c49a4e399855"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles and similarity","lesson_subtopic":"AA similarity","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 330\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Triangle ACB with D between C and B; specified equal angles\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"420\" height=\"330\" fill=\"white\"/><line x1=\"75.00\" y1=\"50.00\" x2=\"75.00\" y2=\"260.00\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"75.00\" y1=\"260.00\" x2=\"355.00\" y2=\"260.00\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"355.00\" y1=\"260.00\" x2=\"75.00\" y2=\"50.00\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"75.00\" y1=\"50.00\" x2=\"232.50\" y2=\"260.00\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"75.00\" cy=\"50.00\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"61.00\" y=\"40.00\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">A</text><circle cx=\"75.00\" cy=\"260.00\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"61.00\" y=\"282.00\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">C</text><circle cx=\"355.00\" cy=\"260.00\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"355.00\" y=\"282.00\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">B</text><circle cx=\"232.50\" cy=\"260.00\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#1264a3\"/><text x=\"232.50\" y=\"282.00\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">D</text><text x=\"240.00\" y=\"38.00\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15263d\">∠CAD = ∠ABC</text><line x1=\"75.00\" y1=\"244.00\" x2=\"91.00\" y2=\"244.00\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"91.00\" y1=\"244.00\" x2=\"91.00\" y2=\"260.00\" stroke=\"#243e5c\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg>","figure_required":true,"figure_caption":"Instructor-created diagram from the explicit mathematical givens; not a recovered source image."},"replaces":["75a90933-fefc-5852-a2e5-c49a4e399855"]},{"id":"5dd53760-0cb1-5181-a2f5-157ec53ccdfd","code":"decreasing-cubic","track_id":"est2","topic":"Functions and transformations","difficulty":"medium","type":"mcq","stem":"The graph shows f(x) = x³ − 6x² + 9x. On which listed interval is f strictly decreasing?","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"−5 < x < 1"},{"key":"B","text":"1 < x < 3"},{"key":"C","text":"x > 3"},{"key":"D","text":"x < 3"}],"correct":"B","explanation":"B. The graph falls from its local maximum (1,4) to its local minimum (3,0), so it decreases on 1 < x < 3. Independently, f′(x) = 3(x − 1)(x − 3), which is negative exactly between 1 and 3 and positive outside. No calculus is needed to read the plotted interval.","assets":{"source":"Eng Abdelrahman Ghoneem — reviewed instructor adaptation","source_question_id":"GH-REVIEW-20261005-decreasing-cubic","source_ids_adapted":["e66b07e7-ae80-5b66-a025-bb6f27c972a6"],"content_origin":"instructor_adaptation","adaptation_reason":"Clarify an ambiguous source item in an explicitly authored adaptation.","curriculum_lesson":"Functions and transformations","lesson_subtopic":"Increasing and decreasing intervals","lesson_taxonomy_version":"20260929","verified_release":"20261005-reviewed-exam-versions","answer_review_status":"independently_solved","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Grap